huawei the world's leading manufacturer  of 5g network gear  a u.s campaign seeks to have allies  around the world dump the supplier for  security reasons  europe has become the focal point of  that campaign and in particular  germany in the high-tech city of  shenzhen where huawei is headquartered  5g is already a reality chinese state  television runs footage of transmission  mass under construction  huawei has become the world's largest  network gear supplier  and was planning to supply its kit for  5g networks around the world  but the us has banned its companies from  doing business with huawei  much to the annoyance of officials in  beijing  what the us has done shows clearly that  the market economy and fair competition  principle it claims to champion is  nothing but a fig leaf  this practice violates the rules of  international trade  disrupts global supply chains and will  inevitably damage america's national  interests  and image washington is calling on the  world to make a decision  against china last week u.s secretary of  state mike pompeo toured europe  and called for huawei to be banned from  5g networks because of espionage fears  europe is divided on how to approach  huawei some countries are lining up with  the us  and restricting huawei are not using its  5g technology at all  others are using huawei's 5g gear  and others are still undecided  the eurozone's biggest economy germany  has so far avoided the decision  it feels caught in the middle but the  usa and china are important trading  partners  berlin doesn't want to ruffle anyone's  feathers  and for more on this i'm joined by  isabelle skereka she's a cyber security  and digital policy researcher  at esmt berlin isabel thanks for joining  us  should europe be concerned should europe  be concerned about huawei as a network  supplier what do you think  i think europe should think about  dependencies in the long term and in  that sense yes it should be concerned  about who it chooses to equip  5g networks strategically because those  networks are critical infrastructures  for the next 20 years and will determine  the fate of our future industry  moreover there are european suppliers  who would be available as well  so we might rather be dependent on  european suppliers than chinese ones  and there are also some security risks  about huawei which are not completely  confirmed  but the laws in china allow the chinese  government to take  influence over chinese companies and  security agencies can force it  potentially to cooperate  on espionage or sabotage all right so  some good technical points about using  huawei  as a supplier is that technical  discussion now being dwarfed  by a larger political discussion and  this tug of war between the u.s and  china  yes it's a very political discussion  it's also a geopolitical discussion  china and the us are basically fighting  over  dominance geopolitical dominance in the  world the u.s  initiative is certainly also an  initiative to preserve  its first position in  the global game so to say so europe  now caught in between really needs to  find its own position  geopolitically as well as  industrially and the focus is really  falling on germany  can berlin really afford to sit on the  finch on the fence rather on this issue  for much longer  no berlin can absolutely not afford to  sit on the fence  it has to take a decision it needs to  act together  with its european allies and partners  because  a sole national decision will not have  as much weight  as a european position this is why the  european union has already initiated  a strategic process last year that  germany is part of  and so in that sense germany now  needs to not only make a technical  decision about technical security of  networks  but also position itself within this  larger geopolitical debate  on the basis of its value european  values  all right we'll have to leave it there  for now isabel skierka with esmt berlin  thank you very much for your analysis  thank you
 

